<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Malavikagnimitram</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Malavikagnimitram is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> play by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalidasa">Kalidasa</a>, probably the earliest of his three surviving dramas. Set at the court of the Shunga king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnimitra">Agnimitra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a>, it is a court comedy about the king's love for Malavika, a maid of the queen who proves to be a princess. It also mentions a war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidarbha">Vidarbha</a> and Pushyamitra's horse sacrifice.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The title means Mālavikā and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnimitra">Agnimitra</a>. The play draws on events of the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pushyamitra-shunga">Pushyamitra Shunga</a>, Agnimitra's father, and is a drama in five acts set in Vidisa and its neighbourhood, with King Agnimitra as its hero and Mālavikā, a princess in the service of the chief queen Dharini, as its heroine. It opens with an invocation of Siva, after which the stage-manager and his assistant announce the play, and it contains an elaborate exposition of a theory of music and acting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The king has seen Mālavikā in a group portrait and the queen keeps her from his sight. With the help of his jester, the Vidushaka Gautama, he provokes a quarrel between the dancing-masters Ganadasa and Haradatta, and a contest between their pupils lets him watch Mālavikā dance. Later, sent by Dharini to touch an Asoka tree with her foot to make it blossom, she is overheard confessing her love, and the jealous second queen Iravati breaks in on the lovers.</p><p>Dharini then has Mālavikā and her friend Vakulavalika locked in a cellar, to be released only on sight of the queen's snake-seal ring. The jester pretends to be bitten by a serpent, obtains the ring for his cure and frees them. In the last act captive maids brought back from the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidarbha">Vidarbha</a> recognise Mālavikā as the lost sister of Madhavasena; news comes that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnimitra">Agnimitra</a>'s son Vasumitra has won a victory while guarding his grandfather's sacrificial horse, and the queen gives Mālavikā to the king.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The play ends with the customary Bharatavakya, here a wish for peace and happiness among the king's subjects. In 1929 Dadasaheb Phalke adapted it as an Indian silent film, Malvikagni Mitra, and Kamlesh Pandey retold it as a comic in the 569th issue of the Indian comic book series Amar Chitra Katha.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mālavikāgnimitram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kalidasa's play <b>Malavikagnimitram</b> is set at the court of Agnimitra in Vidisha, where the Heliodorus pillar records a Greek envoy honouring Vasudeva.</blockquote><a href="/state/shunga-empire">Shunga Empire</a></li></ul></section>
